<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Labotsibeni Mdluli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1859–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Labotsibeni Gwamile Mdluli, Gwamile, Labotsibeni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Labotsibeni Mdluli was queen mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwane-v">Ngwane V</a> and grandmother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-ii">Sobhuza II</a>. She acted as regent during her son's minority and, after his death in 1899, governed for her grandson until 1921. Through the years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> and British rule she defended Swazi land rights, sent a deputation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and promoted modern schooling for the royal family and the nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labotsibeni-mdluli.jpg" alt="Labotsibeni Mdluli" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N.N./National Archives · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Labotsibeni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Labotsibeni, also known as Gwamile, was born at Luhlekweni in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>, the daughter of Matsanjana Mdluli of the high-ranking Mdluli clan; she was named after the people of Tsibeni, whom her father was fighting for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii">Mswati II</a> at the time. After her father's death around 1870 her uncle, Chief Mvelase, brought her to the royal court of Ludzidzini, where she served the old queen mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thandile">Tsandzile Ndwandwe</a>. Soon after 1874 she became a wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbandzeni">Mbandzeni</a>, whose land concessions to whites after the Barberton gold rush were to be the subject of litigation for twenty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When her eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwane-v">Bhunu</a> was chosen as heir in 1889, she became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndlovukati">Ndlovukati</a>, or queen mother, at first second to the regent Tibati. By 1894 she was the stronger of the two, opposing the convention that set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> protectorate in 1895, and she outargued Transvaal leaders such as Piet Joubert and the British consuls. After Bhunu died on 10 December 1899, she ruled for his infant son Mona and, during the South African War, was largely successful in keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a> out of the fighting.</p><p>She protested against the administration set up in 1903 and 1904 and against the land partition proclaimed in 1907, which divided the country between the Swazi nation, the white concessionaires and the British crown. The resident commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-coryndon">Robert Coryndon</a> could not win support to depose her, and later called her a woman of extraordinary diplomatic ability. Her son Prince Malunge led the deputation that met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in February 1908, and the two helped fund Abantu-Batho, the newspaper of the South African Natives National Congress, founded in 1912. She insisted that Mona receive the best schooling open to a black person, sent him to Lovedale in 1916, and handed him authority on 22 December 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Labotsibeni died after a long illness at Zombodze on 15 December 1925 and was buried there. The Times called her the best known native woman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> for two generations, and T. D. Mweli Skota's African Yearly Register described her as a good, wise and tactful ruler. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> is still affectionately called kaGogo eSwatini, the grandmother's Eswatini, in her honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Labotsibeni Mdluli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The regent <b>Labotsibeni Mdluli</b> governed from 1899 to 1921 for her grandson Sobhuza II, who then reigned until 1982, one of the longest reigns in recorded history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The queen regent <b>Labotsibeni</b> led opposition until Sobhuza II came of age in 1921; he petitioned the Privy Council and later bought back land through a national fund.</blockquote><a href="/state/swaziland-protectorate">Swaziland Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Zombodze. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.23,31.34&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Zombodze on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5374 3162 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Labotsibeni Mdluli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5824 3432h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5824 3432h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.23,31.34&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
